<!--
  Beat 10 of the flagship, on the `Assertion-Evidence` layout — and the slide that carries the
  proof's load-bearing claim about figures.

  The chart is `deck/public/redundant-deuteranomaly.png`, the same file the Slidev deck shows: drawn
  by `legible.figures.multi_series` from `themes/kuleuven.json`, simulated with the validator's own
  Machado 2009 code, and imported here as a picture. It is not redrawn as a native PowerPoint chart,
  and that is the decision the whole colour mapping rests on — `series-1 … series-3` never need a
  theme slot because they never leave the image. Regenerate the figure, repack, and this slide is
  current; there is no second copy of the data to update.

  Every line in it carries a dash and a marker of its own, which is `never-sole-channel` doing the
  work grayscale relies on.

  The picture takes the evidence placeholder (`p:ph idx="1"`), so it is the evidence rather than
  something laid on top of it, and the caption spans the pane under it the way the theme's `Figure`
  component sets it: dense, because a caption labels the evidence and is not the evidence.
-->
